  "name": "carousel",
  "description": "Each side owns a private one-way lift into the enemy's half. A White piece that ends its move on b3, in front of White's pawns, comes out one step beyond c5, in the direction it was travelling, capturing there as usual; Black's lift mirrors it, in at b6 and out beside c4. To the other side a lift is solid, and nobody moves one. The lifts interlock: each mouth sits beside the other's exit, so where your raiders leave is next to where the enemy's arrive, and a pawn can ride its lift on the first move into the enemy's third row. The armies are Los Alamos chess's (chess without bishops, castling or a pawn's double step, the first chess a computer played, in 1956), on a board six wide and eight tall whose side edges are joined, so a piece may reach a lift from either side and choose which side of the exit it comes out on.",
